spawn-fcgi installed-package smoke test
=======================================

spawn-fcgi opens a listening Unix or TCP socket and then starts a FastCGI
application with that socket on file descriptor 0.  These tests use
/usr/bin/true as a harmless stand-in application.  It exits immediately, but
a successful command confirms that spawn-fcgi created the listening socket and
started the child process.

# Show the installed version and enabled features.
/usr/bin/spawn-fcgi -v

# Create a Unix listening socket and start the child without forking (-n).
rm -f /var/tmp/spawn-fcgi-smoke.sock
umask 022
/usr/bin/spawn-fcgi -n -s /var/tmp/spawn-fcgi-smoke.sock -- /usr/bin/true
test -S /var/tmp/spawn-fcgi-smoke.sock && echo "Unix socket creation: PASS"
stat -c 'Default socket mode: %a (expected 640)' /var/tmp/spawn-fcgi-smoke.sock
rm -f /var/tmp/spawn-fcgi-smoke.sock

# Confirm that -M can set an explicit Unix-socket mode.
/usr/bin/spawn-fcgi -n -s /var/tmp/spawn-fcgi-smoke.sock -M 0666 -- /usr/bin/true
stat -c 'Explicit socket mode: %a (expected 666)' /var/tmp/spawn-fcgi-smoke.sock
rm -f /var/tmp/spawn-fcgi-smoke.sock

# Bind IPv4 and IPv6 loopback sockets and start the child application.
/usr/bin/spawn-fcgi -n -a 127.0.0.1 -p 41001 -- /usr/bin/true && echo "IPv4 bind and child launch: PASS"
/usr/bin/spawn-fcgi -n -a ::1 -p 41002 -- /usr/bin/true && echo "IPv6 bind and child launch: PASS"

If either TCP port is already in use, repeat that command with another high
port number.  These checks test socket creation and process launching; they do
not send a FastCGI request.
